\title{Quantum Error Correction from Homological Algebra:\\ CSS Codes as Cohomology}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop the exact dictionary between Calderbank--Shor--Steane (CSS) quantum
error-correcting codes and the homology of chain complexes. A CSS code is,
algebraically, a length-two segment of a chain complex
$A \xrightarrow{d_2} B \xrightarrow{d_1} C$ with $d_1 \circ d_2 = 0$; the
physical qubits are a basis of the middle space $B$, and the \emph{logical
qubits} are precisely the middle homology $H = \ker d_1 / \im d_2$. We prove two
structural dimension identities that drive every CSS/HQECC computation: the
\textbf{dimension formula} $k + \rank d_1 + \rank d_2 = \dim B$ (the CSS count
$k = n - \rank(H_X) - \rank(H_Z)$), and the \textbf{Euler identity}
$\beta_0 + \dim B = \dim(\ker d_1) + \dim C$, which for a graph complex reads
$V - E = \beta_0 - \beta_1$. Both are established additively over an arbitrary
field, avoiding truncated subtraction and making them field-agnostic.
Specializing to the graph case yields the homological quantum error-correcting
code $\HQECC(G)$, whose logical dimension equals the circuit rank
$k = \beta_1(G) = E - V + \beta_0$. Applying this to the $n$-dimensional
hypercube graph $Q_n$, we obtain the exact closed form
$\beta_1(Q_n) = 2^{n-1}(n-2) + 1$, and we prove that the folklore ``one logical
qubit'' law holds \emph{only} at $n = 2$ (the $4$-cycle): for all $n \ge 3$ the
code encodes at least five logical qubits, with $Q_4, Q_6, Q_8$ encoding
$17, 129, 769$ qubits respectively.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Quantum error correction is the foundation on which scalable quantum computation
rests. Among the most influential families of quantum codes are the CSS codes of
Calderbank, Shor, and Steane, built from a pair of classical binary linear codes
whose parity-check structures are mutually compatible. A recurring observation is
that the CSS construction has the exact shape of a homological computation. The
purpose of this paper is to make that precise, to prove the structural identities
it entails, and to test it on the hypercube graphs.

A CSS code is specified by two parity-check matrices $H_X$ and $H_Z$ over $\FF_2$
satisfying $H_X H_Z^{\mathsf{T}} = 0$. Reading $H_Z^{\mathsf{T}}$ as a map $d_2$
into the space of physical qubits and $H_X$ as a map $d_1$ out of it, the
compatibility condition becomes $d_1 \circ d_2 = 0$: a two-term chain complex.
The number of encoded logical qubits, classically
$k = n - \rank H_X - \rank H_Z$, is exactly the dimension of the middle homology
group. We prove this equivalence and extract two accounting identities that
underlie all downstream computations, then instantiate the theory for graphs,
where the construction becomes the homological quantum error-correcting code
$\HQECC(G)$.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $K$ is a field. All vector spaces are $K$-vector spaces; for a linear
map $f$ we write $\ker f$, $\im f$, $\rank f = \dim \im f$, and $\dim$ for
$\dim_K$.

\begin{definition}[CSS chain complex]
A \emph{CSS complex} is a triple of $K$-vector spaces with two linear maps
$A \xrightarrow{d_2} B \xrightarrow{d_1} C$ satisfying the \emph{chain
condition} $d_1 \circ d_2 = 0$. The middle space $B$ is the \emph{physical
space}; a basis of $B$ indexes the physical qubits. The map $d_1$ plays the role
of the $X$-type parity check $H_X$, and $d_2$ that of $H_Z^{\mathsf{T}}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cycles and boundaries]
The \emph{cycle space} is $Z = \ker d_1 \subseteq B$, and the \emph{boundary
space} is $B_\partial = \im d_2 \subseteq B$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Boundaries are cycles]\label{lem:bdry}
$B_\partial \subseteq Z$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $b = d_2(a) \in \im d_2$, then $d_1(b) = (d_1 \circ d_2)(a) = 0$, so
$b \in \ker d_1 = Z$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Logical space / middle homology]
The \emph{logical space} is the quotient
$H = Z / B_\partial = \ker d_1 / \im d_2$. The \emph{number of logical qubits}
is $k = \dim H$; the quotient is well-defined by Lemma~\ref{lem:bdry}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Zeroth homology]
The \emph{zeroth homology} is $H^0 = C / \im d_1$, with $\beta_0 = \dim H^0$.
For a graph complex $\beta_0$ is the number of connected components.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Graph complex / HQECC]
Given a finite graph $G = (V,E)$, its \emph{graph complex} over $K$ takes
$B = K^E$, $C = K^V$, $A = 0$ (so $d_2 = 0$), and $d_1 = \partial$ the incidence
map. The resulting CSS code is the \emph{homological quantum error-correcting
code} $\HQECC(G)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Main results}

\subsection{The structural dimension identities}

\begin{proposition}[Splitting off the boundaries]\label{prop:split}
For a CSS complex with $\dim B < \infty$, $\;k + \rank d_2 = \dim Z.$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
By the first isomorphism theorem, $\dim(Z/B_\partial) + \dim B_\partial = \dim Z$.
The inclusion $Z \hookrightarrow B$ identifies $\im d_2$ inside $Z$ with $\im d_2$
inside $B$, so $\dim B_\partial = \rank d_2$, giving $k + \rank d_2 = \dim Z$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Rank--nullity on $d_1$]\label{prop:rn}
For a CSS complex with $\dim B < \infty$, $\;\dim Z + \rank d_1 = \dim B.$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Rank--nullity for $d_1 : B \to C$, since $Z = \ker d_1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[CSS dimension formula]\label{thm:dim}
For a CSS complex with $\dim B < \infty$,
\[ k + \rank d_1 + \rank d_2 = \dim B, \]
equivalently $k = \dim B - \rank d_1 - \rank d_2$, the CSS count
$k = n - \rank(H_X) - \rank(H_Z)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Add Propositions~\ref{prop:split} and~\ref{prop:rn} and cancel $\dim Z$:
$k + \rank d_2 + \rank d_1 = \dim Z + \rank d_1 = \dim B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Euler identity]\label{thm:euler}
For a CSS complex with $\dim B, \dim C < \infty$,
\[ \beta_0 + \dim B = \dim Z + \dim C. \]
For a graph complex ($\dim B = E$, $\dim C = V$) this reads $\beta_0 + E =
\beta_1 + V$, i.e. $V - E = \beta_0 - \beta_1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Rank--nullity for $H^0 = C / \im d_1$ gives $\beta_0 + \rank d_1 = \dim C$.
Substituting $\rank d_1 = \dim B - \dim Z$ from Proposition~\ref{prop:rn} yields
$\beta_0 + \dim B - \dim Z = \dim C$, which rearranges to the claim.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The graph specialization}

\begin{theorem}[Logical dimension of a graph code]\label{thm:graph}
For the graph complex of $G$ (so $d_2 = 0$),
\[ k = \dim Z, \qquad k + V = E + \beta_0, \qquad k = E - V + \beta_0 =
\beta_1(G). \]
If $G$ is connected then $\beta_0 = 1$ and $k = E - V + 1$, the circuit rank.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
When $d_2 = 0$, $\im d_2 = 0$, so $H \cong Z$ and $k = \dim Z$
(Proposition~\ref{prop:split}). The count $k + V = E + \beta_0$ is
Theorem~\ref{thm:euler} with $\dim Z = k$. Rearranging and using connectivity
gives $k = E - V + 1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The hypercube homological code}

The $n$-dimensional hypercube graph $Q_n$ has vertex set $\{0,1\}^n$, edges
between strings differing in one coordinate; it is connected with
$V(Q_n) = 2^n$ and $E(Q_n) = n \cdot 2^{n-1}$.

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:closed}
For $n \ge 1$, $\HQECC(Q_n)$ encodes
\[ k = \beta_1(Q_n) = E - V + 1 = n\cdot 2^{n-1} - 2^n + 1 = 2^{n-1}(n-2) + 1 \]
logical qubits.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:graph}, $k = E - V + 1$. Substituting $E = n\cdot 2^{n-1}$
and $V = 2^n = 2\cdot 2^{n-1}$ gives $2^{n-1}(n-2) + 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The ``one qubit'' law holds only at $n=2$]\label{thm:one}
For $n \ge 1$, $\beta_1(Q_n) = 1$ if and only if $n = 2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\beta_1(Q_n) = 1 \iff 2^{n-1}(n-2) = 0 \iff n = 2$, since $2^{n-1} > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Failure for $n \ge 3$]\label{thm:fail}
For $n \ge 3$, $\beta_1(Q_n) \ge 5$; in particular the hypercube code encodes
strictly more than one logical qubit.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $n \ge 3$, $2^{n-1} \ge 4$ and $n - 2 \ge 1$, so
$\beta_1(Q_n) = 2^{n-1}(n-2) + 1 \ge 5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Test cases]
$\beta_1(Q_4) = 17$, $\beta_1(Q_6) = 129$, $\beta_1(Q_8) = 769$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$2^3(2)+1 = 17$; $2^5(4)+1 = 129$; $2^7(6)+1 = 769$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Graph versus cell complex]
The folklore ``one qubit'' claim conflates the hypercube \emph{graph} (a
one-dimensional complex whose first homology is the cycle space of dimension
$2^{n-1}(n-2)+1$) with the hypercube \emph{cell complex} (the filled solid,
whose middle homology can be small). The single-qubit law is correct only for
$Q_2$, the $4$-cycle.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{description}
\item[Algorithm A (rank data).] Given $d_1, d_2$, compute
$r_1 = \rank d_1$, $r_2 = \rank d_2$ by Gaussian elimination and return
$k = \dim B - r_1 - r_2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:dim}); complexity $O(n^3)$.
\item[Algorithm B (graph parameters).] Compute $V$, $E$, and $\beta_0$ by
union--find, then return $k = E - V + \beta_0$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:graph});
complexity $O((V+E)\,\alpha(V))$.
\item[Algorithm C (hypercube closed form).] Return $2^{n-1}(n-2)+1$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}); $O(1)$ arithmetic operations.
\end{description}

\section{Applications}

Every simplicial or cell complex yields a CSS code whose logical dimension, rate,
and distance are topological invariants: the Betti number, the
Euler-characteristic-controlled rate, and the systole. Surface and toric codes
are graph homological codes; the identities here compute their logical dimension
uniformly. Theorem~\ref{thm:euler} turns the code rate $k/n$ into a combinatorial
ratio $1 - (V-\beta_0)/E$ for graph codes.

\section{Discussion}

Stating rank--nullity additively makes every identity valid without side
conditions and field-agnostic; nothing uses the characteristic, so the theorems
describe qudit CSS codes over any prime field. Insisting on an exact chain
complex forces one to specify which space is meant, which is where the hypercube
folklore went astray.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Distance of the hypercube code.} We conjecture $\HQECC(Q_n)$ has
minimum distance equal to the girth $4$ for all $n \ge 2$, hence does not achieve
the quantum Singleton bound $d = 2^{n/2}$ for $n \ge 5$.
\item \textbf{Euler characteristic as rate obstruction.} The rate
$k/n = 1 - (V-1)/E$ is maximized by bouquets and minimized by trees.
\item \textbf{Realizability.} Every pair $k \le n$ is realized by a length-two
chain complex with $\dim B = n$, $\dim H = k$.
\item \textbf{Cohomological duality.} $\ker d_1 / \im d_2$ and
$\ker d_2^{\mathsf{T}} / \im d_1^{\mathsf{T}}$ have equal dimension, a self-dual
invariant.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Quantum error correction, in its CSS incarnation, is homology made concrete:
physical qubits are the middle of a chain complex, logical qubits its middle
homology, and code parameters topological invariants. The CSS dimension formula
and the Euler identity furnish a complete accounting of the logical dimension;
the hypercube case study shows $\HQECC(Q_n)$ encodes $2^{n-1}(n-2)+1$ logical
qubits, refuting the single-qubit folklore for all $n \ge 3$ while confirming it
uniquely at $n = 2$.

